{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Three years it shall be to you arelah.</b> Many commentators have explained the reason of this commandment: because it was a practice of the Emorites to devise artifices to hasten the fruit of the tree before its time. And so the Rambam wrote in the Guide for the Perplexed. But what appears close to reason is to say that this commandment is in order to make a remembrance of the work of Creation, for they were all created on the first day and each one emerged on its day. And likewise all the vegetation of the earth was covered until the third day, and even though it sprouted on the third day, nevertheless it was not visible until the fourth day, on which the luminaries were hung, which ripen all the fruits, for all of them depend on the sun and the moon, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:14): ״מִמֶּגֶד תְּבוּאֹת שָׁמֶשׁ וּמִמֶּגֶד גֶּרֶשׁ יְרָחִים.״ It is found that they were covered and invisible for eating until the fourth day. Therefore He commanded to make a remembrance of the work of Creation, that they be covered and sealed for three years, for the matter of orlah is covering and sealing. And on the fourth day, even though their ripening was complete, nevertheless there was not yet in the world any living creature, and there was no mouth here to eat them — as if their emergence on the fourth day were only to give thanks and praise to their Creator who created them; therefore it says here, as a remembrance of the work of Creation: ״וּבַשָּׁנָה הָרְבִיעִת יִהְיֶה כָּל פִּרְיוֹ קֹדֶשׁ הִלּוּלִים לַה׳.״ And on the fifth day some of the living creatures were created, which needed the food of vegetation; therefore it says: ״וּבַשָּׁנָה הַחֲמִישִׁית תֹּאכְלוּ אֶת פִּרְיוֹ.״ And it does not say 'kol piryo' in the manner that it says ״וּבַשָּׁנָה הָרְבִיעִת יִהְיֶה כָּל פִּרְיוֹ קֹדֶשׁ,״ to teach that one should not eat all its fruit but should give of them all also to the poor person and to the stranger, because it mentioned leket, shichechah, and pe'ah for produce and not for trees, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Jerusalemite Pe'ah 1:4): from where do we derive trees? It is derived by saying: ״שָׂדְךָ,״ therefore it does not say 'kol.' And regarding the trait of charity it says: ״לְהוֹסִיף לָכֶם תְּבוּאָתוֹ,״ for 'there is one who scatters and yet increases' (Proverbs 11:24). And because there are those who seek a guarantor and assurance for this, therefore it says: ״אֲנִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״ — trustworthy to pay."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:41.269154920Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}